<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Summary of the datasets used in the experimental evaluation
retrieved from the UCI Machine Learning Repository <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Asuncion et al., 2007</a>)</cite>. For each dataset, we report the total number of instances and the number of target classes.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Models</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the theoretical insights of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Advani et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, we employed fully connected neural networks with a single hidden layer and ReLU activation functions, in order to preserve architectural simplicity and experimental controllability while exploring different generalization regimes. Indeed, as shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Advani et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, generalization behavior depends critically on the ratio between the number of trainable parameters and the number of training samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, we define a parameter-to-sample ratio <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to an equal number of model parameters and samples, while values below or above <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> indicate under- and over-parameterized regimes, respectively. Thus, the use of a shallow architecture allows us to systematically explore these regimes by varying the number of hidden units so as to control the total number of trainable parameters relative to the size of the training dataset. In our experiments, we consider the values <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\{0.3,0.5,0.7,0.8,1,1.2,5,10,50\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\{0.3,0.5,0.7,0.8,1,1.2,5,10,50\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that we deliberately focus on shallow neural networks with a single hidden layer, as our goal is not to achieve state-of-the-art performance, but to isolate and analyze the effect of validation criteria on model selection. Deeper architectures introduce multiple additional factors–such as hierarchical representations, layer-wise implicit regularization, and complex optimization dynamics–that can confound the interpretation of validation-based selection mechanisms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By adopting a controlled shallow setting, we are able to systematically vary the parameter-to-sample ratio and explore different generalization regimes while keeping architectural and optimization-related effects to a minimum. This choice enables a clearer assessment of how different validation criteria influence model selection, independently of depth-related phenomena.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Adopted losses</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Cross-entropy: cross-entropy loss, widely used in classification tasks, emerges naturally from the principle of maximum likelihood estimation under the assumption that the model outputs a categorical distribution over the classes. It is defined as</p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\ell_{\text{CE}}=-\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{k=1}^{K}t_{k}^{(i)}\log\left(m_{k}^{(i)}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\text{CE}}=-\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{k=1}^{K}t_{k}^{(i)}\log\left(m_{k}^{(i)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐭</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the one-hot encoded target vector <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{(i)}\in\{0,1\}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{(i)}\in\{0,1\}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> of the actual label <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}^{(i)}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}^{(i)}=1</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=y^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=y^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}^{(i)}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}^{(i)}=0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise, and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}^{(i)}=(m_{1}^{(i)},\dots,m_{K}^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}^{(i)}=(m_{1}^{(i)},\dots,m_{K}^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math> is the class output probability distribution of the model <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> on the input <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leng et al. (2022)</a></cite> introduce <em id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">PolyLoss</em>, a polynomial reparameterization of cross-entropy obtained via its Taylor expansion around the correct-class confidence. Denoting by <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{y^{(i)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{y^{(i)}}</annotation></semantics></math> the predicted probability for the true class of sample <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, the loss takes the form</p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\text{PolyLoss}}\;=\;\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}\alpha_{j}\,\big(1-m_{y^{(i)}}\big)^{j}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mtext>PolyLoss</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\text{PolyLoss}}\;=\;\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}\alpha_{j}\,\big(1-m_{y^{(i)}}\big)^{j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">with coefficients <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\alpha_{j}\}_{j\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\alpha_{j}\}_{j\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> to be tuned. In its natural (infinite) form, PolyLoss is impractical and does not consistently outperform standard cross-entropy. To address this, the authors propose a simplified, first-order truncation,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\text{Poly-1}}\;=\;-\log m_{y^{(i)}}\;+\;\epsilon\,\big(1-m_{y^{(i)}}\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mtext>Poly-1</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\text{Poly-1}}\;=\;-\log m_{y^{(i)}}\;+\;\epsilon\,\big(1-m_{y^{(i)}}\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">controlled by a scalar hyperparameter <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The C-Loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singh and Principe, 2010</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singh et al., 2014</a>)</cite> is a surrogate for the 0–1 loss built from the <em id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">correntropy</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Santamaría et al., 2006</a>)</cite> between true labels and model scores. Unlike cross-entropy, the C-Loss can be more robust to outliers and label noise. In binary classification problems where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{(i)}\in\{-1,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{(i)}\in\{-1,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and single output <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{(i)}=M(\mathbf{x}^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{(i)}=M(\mathbf{x}^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math>, it is defined via a positive-definite kernel <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\mathcal{k}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\mathcal{k}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> (typically Gaussian):</p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{{C}}(y^{(i)},m^{(i)})\;=\beta\big(1-\mathcal{k}_{\sigma}(y^{(i)}-m^{(i)})\big)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓀</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{{C}}(y^{(i)},m^{(i)})\;=\beta\big(1-\mathcal{k}_{\sigma}(y^{(i)}-m^{(i)})\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{k}_{\sigma}(u)=\exp\!\Big(-\tfrac{u^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓀</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{k}_{\sigma}(u)=\exp\!\Big(-\tfrac{u^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\Big)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> parameters properly chosen. Multiclass variants can be built by applying the one-class-versus-the-rest strategy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Training and Evaluation Protocol</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Models were trained for a maximum of <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=20,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=20,000</annotation></semantics></math> epochs for each dataset using stochastic gradient descent with a batch size of <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64</annotation></semantics></math> samples. The learning rate was set to <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math> and fixed through all the training epochs. To obtain statistically reliable estimates, all results were computed under a <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>-fold stratified cross-validation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bishop and Bishop, 2023</a>)</cite> scheme.
For each fold, the <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>15</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> of the training set was used for validation set <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Val" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Val</annotation></semantics></math> using stratified sampling
 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bishop and Bishop, 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Prior to each training, all input features were then standardized using <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-score normalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hastie, 2009</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Apicella et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. The normalization parameters (mean and standard deviation) were computed exclusively on the training portion of each fold and subsequently applied to the corresponding validation and test sets, ensuring that no information from the held-out data leaked into the training process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Apicella et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We emphasize that, as above discussed, the objective of this work is not to achieve state-of-the-art performance on these benchmarks, thus we intentionally adopt simple and uniform preprocessing rather than dataset-specific preprocessing prior to each training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For each dataset and each fold, the model was trained while monitoring validation loss <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(Val,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(Val,e)</annotation></semantics></math> and validation accuracy <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{a}(Val,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒶</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{a}(Val,e)</annotation></semantics></math> at every epoch <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>. Model selection was performed based solely on validation criteria, but evaluation was always carried out on the corresponding <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Test" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Test</annotation></semantics></math> fold. Specifically, for each <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Test" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Test</annotation></semantics></math> fold we computed:</p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the test accuracy of the model corresponding to the epoch with the minimum validation loss, denoted as <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{a}({Test},e^{\star}_{\ell,Val})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒶</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{a}({Test},e^{\star}_{\ell,Val})</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the test accuracy of the model corresponding to the epoch with the maximum validation accuracy, denoted as <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{a}({Test},e^{\star}_{\mathcal{a},Val})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒶</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒶</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{a}({Test},e^{\star}_{\mathcal{a},Val})</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the test-optimal accuracy, defined as the maximum test accuracy achieved across all training epochs, denoted as <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\star}_{Test}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\star}_{Test}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F3.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="263.91" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 387.28 263.91" width="387.28"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,263.91) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(58.73,0) translate(0,39.47)"><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -58.73 -39.47)"><g class="ltx_nestedsvg" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0) translate(51.87,0) translate(0,-56.64)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#B0B0B0;--ltx-fill-color:#B0B0B0;--ltx-fg-color:#B0B0B0;" color="#B0B0B0" fill="#B0B0B0" stroke="#B0B0B0" stroke-width="0.4pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 125.76 96.11 L 125.76 320.27 M 153.2 96.11 L 153.2 320.27 M 203.51 96.11 L 203.51 320.27 M 276.68 96.11 L 276.68 320.27"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#B0B0B0;--ltx-fill-color:#B0B0B0;--ltx-fg-color:#B0B0B0;" color="#B0B0B0" fill="#B0B0B0" stroke="#B0B0B0" stroke-width="0.4pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 6.86 132.01 L 276.68 132.01 M 6.86 198.02 L 276.68 198.02 M 6.86 264.03 L 276.68 264.03"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" color="#808080" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.2pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 125.76 90.2 L 125.76 96.11 M 153.2 90.2 L 153.2 96.11 M 203.51 90.2 L 203.51 96.11 M 276.68 90.2 L 276.68 96.11"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" color="#808080" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.2pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0.95 132.01 L 6.86 132.01 M 0.95 198.02 L 6.86 198.02 M 0.95 264.03 L 6.86 264.03"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 6.86 96.11 L 6.86 320.27 L 276.68 320.27 L 276.68 96.11 L 6.86 96.11 Z"></path><g style="--